# visibility demo (negative, and currently a DEVIATION): `helper` is module-private,
# so naming it across the module boundary is specified to be a compile error — and
# this compiler does not enforce it. The build below succeeds and prints 2.
#   zerg build examples/1g/private/main.zg   # specified: error, `helper` is not public
#                                            # today:     builds, and prints 2
# `pub` is parsed and re-export works (see ../reexport); what is missing is the
# refusal. It is marked [deviation] in docs/runtime/package.md — when the refusal
# lands this example stops building, and that failure is the reminder to rewrite it
# as the negative case it was always meant to be.
import "lib"

fn main() {
	print lib.helper() # 2 — specified to be rejected, allowed by this compiler
}
